wed on 10/16/2007.  aIncludes bibliographical references.3 a"This paper explores some of the implications for econometric practice of the principle that people's observed behavior will change when their constraints change.  In dynamic contexts, a proper definition of people's constraints includes among them laws of motion that describe the evolution of the taxes they must pay and the prices of the goods that they buy and sell.  Changes in agents' perceptions of these laws of motion (or constraints) will in general produce changes in the schedules that describe the choices they make as a function of the information that they possess.  Until very recently, received dynamic econometric practice ignored this principle.  The practice of dynamic econometrics should be changed so that it is consistent with the principle that people's rules of choice are influenced by their constraints.  This is a substantial undertaking, and involves major adjustments in the ways that we formulate, estimate, and simulate econometric models"--Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis web site.  aAlso available in print.  aSystem requirements: Adobe Acrobat Reader.  aMode of access: World Wide Web. 0aTime-series analysis. 0aStaff report (Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis. Research Department : Online) ;v58.40uhttp://www.minneapolisfed.org/research/common/pub_detail.cfm?pb_autonum_id=33900791nam a2200193 p 450000500100000004000110001004100080002108200160002908400130004509000230005810000260008124502450010726000980035230000380045065000300048865000100051870000140052870000550054230/8/2024  aDHSPKT  aVie  a629.893223  aĐTĐ-51  a629.893bV872-A596  aVõ Đoàn Việt Anh  aDelta robot phân loại sản phẩm dùng PLC S7-1200:bĐồ án tốt nghiệp ngành Công nghệ kỹ thuật điều khiển và tự động hóa/cVõ Đoàn Việt Anh, Hà Hạt; Nguyễn Phong Lưu (Giảng viên hướng dẫn)  aTP. Hồ Chí Minh:bTrường Đại học Sư phạm Kỹ thuật TP. Hồ Chí Minh,c2024  a73tr.:bminh họa;c30cm, 1 file  aPh